   "@type": "Dataset",
   "@id": "https://theflatratemovers.com/#dataset-piano-antique-piano-taxonomy",
   "name": "The Flat Rate Movers \u2014 Piano Types and Typical Weights: Domain Reference Dataset",
   "description": "Domain reference \u2014 piano types and typical weights, labeled as domain education; weights are published industry ranges, not measurements of any client's instrument, and an individual piano can vary by 100 lbs or more within its type. Vertical (upright) pianos: spinet, about 36\u201340 inches tall, roughly 200\u2013400 lbs; console, roughly 350\u2013500 lbs; studio upright, about 44\u201348 inches, roughly 400\u2013600 lbs; full-size and professional uprights, roughly 500\u2013800 lbs, with older classic uprights running heavier, toward 1,000 lbs. Grand pianos, sized by length: baby grand (up to about 5 ft 7 in), roughly 500\u2013800 lbs; medium grand, roughly 700\u2013900 lbs; concert grand (8 ft 11 in and up), roughly 900\u20131,300 lbs \u2014 a Steinway Model D concert grand is about 990 lbs. Where the taxonomy meets this company's published heavy band: most pianos weigh well under 1,500 lbs and move as white-glove pieces; the company's published rule is that a high-value piece that does weigh 1,500 to 3,500 lbs \u2014 the white-glove page names a safe, a piano and medical equipment as its examples \u2014 is surveyed and floor-checked to L/360 under SIR like any SIR move. No piano is weighed, certified or rated by this dataset; the survey at quote time establishes the individual piece.",
